      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2017 Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          "Some children with intellectual disabilities (ID): experience pain more frequently than children without ID, express their pain differently, and are incapable of providing self-reports. No research has examined disability and pain-related beliefs of respite workers (RW) and their relations to pain assessment and management decisions for children with ID.",
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
            "content": "(1) compare disability and pain-related beliefs between RW and a sample with little experience in ID; (2) determine whether individuals' beliefs and personal characteristics are related to pain assessment and management decisions."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "PARTICIPANTS",
            "content": "Fifty-six RW (aged: 18-67 years, Mage=33.37, 46 female) and 141 emerging adults (aged: 18-31 years, Mage=19.67, 137 female)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
            "Label": "PROCEDURE/MEASURES",
            "content": "In an online survey, participants responded to six vignettes depicting pain in children with ID, and completed measures of pain and disability-related beliefs."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "RESULTS/DISCUSSION",
            "content": "Compared to those without experience, RW held more positive disability-related beliefs, t(192)=4.23, p<0.001. Participants' pain-related beliefs (e.g., sensitivity to pain) differed depending on severity of the child's ID and participant group. Participants' pain-related beliefs predicted care decisions. Results provide initial insight into RW pain-related beliefs about children with ID, and a basic understanding of the relations among pain beliefs, personal characteristics and pain-related decisions."
          }
        ]
      },
